OpenSSH 10.6p1 for OPENSTEP

This package installs the OpenSSH binaries and config files, but it does
not create host keys or the privilege-separation account automatically.

After installing the package, do the following on the OPENSTEP host.

1. Generate host keys

   /usr/local/bin/ssh-keygen -A

2. Create the privilege-separation directory

   mkdir /var
   mkdir /var/empty
   chown root /var/empty
   chmod 755 /var/empty

   /var/empty must be owned by root and must not be group- or
   world-writable.

3. Create the sshd privilege-separation group and user

   This host does not provide groupadd/useradd. Use NetInfo via niutil.
   The example below uses gid 67 and uid 67. First check that these IDs
   are unused with niutil -list . /groups gid and niutil -list . /users uid.

   niutil -create . /groups/sshd
   niutil -createprop . /groups/sshd name sshd
   niutil -createprop . /groups/sshd passwd '*'
   niutil -createprop . /groups/sshd gid 67
   niutil -createprop . /groups/sshd users sshd

   niutil -create . /users/sshd
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd name sshd
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd passwd '*'
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd uid 67
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd gid 67
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd realname 'sshd privsep'
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd home /var/empty
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd shell /bin/false
   niutil -createprop . /users/sshd _writers_passwd root

4. Verify the setup

   niutil -read . /groups/sshd
   niutil -read . /users/sshd
   /usr/local/sbin/sshd -t

5. Debug startup if needed

   /usr/local/sbin/sshd -D -ddd

6. Start sshd automatically at boot (/etc/rc.local)

   OPENSTEP runs /etc/rc.local as root after starting network services.
   Back up that file, then append this block without replacing its
   existing contents:

   # Start OpenSSH after the standard network services.
   if [ -x /usr/local/sbin/sshd ]; then
       if /usr/local/sbin/sshd -t; then
           /usr/local/sbin/sshd
       fi
   fi

   Check shell syntax with /bin/sh -n /etc/rc.local. To start the server
   immediately, run /usr/local/sbin/sshd as root after sshd -t succeeds.
   sshd backgrounds itself; do not use -D or -i for this startup method.
   Verify the listener with netstat -an | grep '\.22 ' and connect with
   ssh me@192.168.1.76 (substitute your username and host address).

   Use either this standalone startup method or inetd below, not both.

7. Alternative: start sshd from inetd (/etc/inetd.conf)

   OpenSSH supports inetd mode via sshd -i.

   On this host, NetInfo is used for service lookup. Create the ssh service
   entry in NetInfo as root:

   niutil -create . /services/ssh
   niutil -createprop . /services/ssh name ssh
   niutil -createprop . /services/ssh port 22
   niutil -createprop . /services/ssh protocol tcp

   If you also want a matching flat-file entry, add this line to
   /etc/services:

   ssh        22/tcp

   Add an inetd entry like this to /etc/inetd.conf:

   ssh	stream	tcp	nowait	root	/usr/local/sbin/sshd	sshd -i

   Then, as root, make inetd reread /etc/inetd.conf by sending it SIGHUP.
   For example:

   ps auxww | grep '(inetd)'
   kill -HUP <inetd-pid>

   If inetd is not already running, start it as root with:

   /usr/etc/inetd

   In inetd mode, you will not see a permanent sshd process after boot.
   Instead, verify that inetd is listening on port 22:

   netstat -an | grep '\.22 '

Upgrading from 7.9p1 to 10.6p1

   The host keys, /var/empty, the sshd account and sshd_config from the 7.9p1
   setup are used as they are; nothing above needs to be redone.  What changed:

   - sshd is now two programs.  /usr/local/sbin/sshd listens and starts
     /usr/local/libexec/sshd-session for each connection (and, when it
     authenticates, sshd-auth).  Both are installed by the package and must
     stay where they are.
   - Restart sshd as soon as the package is installed.  A 7.9p1 sshd that is
     still running starts each new connection by running the sshd on disk,
     which no longer understands its arguments, so new logins fail until it
     is restarted.  Test first with a second daemon on a spare port
     (sshd -D -ddd -p 2222) before you stop the old one.  With inetd nothing
     needs restarting: the next connection uses the new binary.
   - Newer defaults: ssh-rsa (SHA-1) signatures are no longer accepted and DSA
     keys are gone.  A client or server that only offers those will refuse to
     connect; use rsa-sha2-256/512, ecdsa or ed25519 keys.
   - ssh-agent cannot check which user connects to its socket, because
     OPENSTEP has no getpeereid() or SO_PEERCRED; keep agent sockets private.
   - Keys are held in ordinary memory (OPENSTEP has no munmap()), and the
     PKCS#11 and FIDO helpers are not built.
